## Efficient customization (mandatory — read `_brand/SKILL.md` first)

House-wide for **every** Customization Studio document (all Proposal types, Budgetary estimate, Scope of work, Capabilities, Sales deck):

- **Mike can click-edit any text.** Fonts/colors/layout stay on CSS. Only LUCI logo images stay non-editable.
- **Populate-in-place.** Never rebuild the document. Never re-emit whole `<section>`s to change a value.
- **Two-folder architecture.** OneDrive "Cursor Branding Files" = source (read-only). `~/Desktop/LUCI Docs/<client-name>/` = working folder (created by `scripts/create-client-workspace.sh`, with symlinks to OneDrive for CSS/assets/scripts). Client HTML lives in `clients/<client-name>-<doc>.html`. Serve from the **working folder root** (the folder that contains `clients/`, `ui_kits/`, `assets/`), never from `clients/` or `ui_kits/sales/`.
- Edit by `[data-studio]` / skill selectors only. Do not invent fine-print or “Addressed to.”
- **Same working file forever.** The LUCI dev server's `POST /__save` writes Mike's typed edits back to the working `.html` file on disk when he clicks Save — so the next agent pass reads the latest version. `*-edited.html` downloads are a last-resort fallback artifact, not the source of truth.
- No accessibility snapshots. Mandatory overflow fit-check after content edits (see `_brand/SKILL.md`).
- Do not link CSS the master does not already link (strips header bands / circuit texture).
- **Download PDF** = `POST /__pdf` on the LUCI dev server (headless Chrome — never `window.print()`, which crashes Cursor's in-editor browser). `patch-sales-pdf-html.py` flattens CSS gradients + mask-images automatically so the PDF doesn't blink in macOS Preview — see `_brand/SKILL.md` → **PDF export — gradient + mask flattening** for the maintenance rule when adding new gradients. **If the PDF button fails**, check `GET /__health` → `pdfDeps` — the most common cause is missing Chrome or Pillow/numpy on Mike's machine. See `_brand/SKILL.md` → **PDF button shows "Failed"?** for diagnosis + fix. Fallback: run `scripts/render-pdf.sh` directly from the terminal.

When a change is Proposal-structure/copy-specific (page order, estimate format, etc.), **ask Jane** whether it should also apply to Budgetary estimate / Scope of work / other studio docs before propagating.

## Click-to-edit in preview

Client HTML keeps `contenteditable="true"` on editable regions (`.doc-edit`, `.deck-edit`, `[data-studio]`). When Cursor previews the file, Mike can click cover lines, prepared-for text, close page copy, etc. and type. Image swaps (client logo, environment photos) are done in HTML or via the Customization Studio form.

- **Preserve** `contenteditable`, `data-studio`, `doc-edit`, and `deck-edit` markers when customizing — do not strip them.
- **Do not** edit locked pages (per template `SKILL.md`).
- When Mike types in the preview, **Save** writes those edits back to the working file on disk automatically (via the dev server's `POST /__save`) — no manual copy/paste, no prompting Cursor. Only if the dev server isn't running does Mike need to ask Cursor to reopen the project.

## Locked pages are locked — no exceptions without confirmation

"Locked" means **no changes of any kind** — not text, images, diagrams, stats, structure, **and not color, styling, spacing, layout, or CSS either.** That covers edits to the HTML, inline `style` attributes, scoped `<style>` blocks in the client file, **and the shared stylesheet files** (`sales-document.css`, `capabilities-document.css`, `brochure.css`, `scope-of-work.css`, `budgetary-estimate.css`). A color-only or "small" styling change to a locked page is still a change to a locked page.

### Pre-edit gate — run before every edit to a client sales doc

Before making any edit, check the target element/selector against the template `SKILL.md` editable-regions list:

- **Target listed as editable** → proceed.
- **Target NOT listed as editable, or IS in a locked list** → **do NOT edit.** Stop and tell the user:

  > "Page X is locked per the `<template>` SKILL.md (protected brand/technical content). Do you want to override that? And if so, should it be **local to this client doc only** or **canonical** (master + shared CSS, propagates to all docs)?"

  Wait for the answer before editing.

A user request to change a locked page is **not** an override by itself — it is the trigger for this pause-and-confirm. Never edit a locked page first and mention it after.

## Open the rendered preview in the editor (automatic — every time)

After customizing the client HTML, **automatically open the rendered doc in Cursor's in-editor (Glass) browser — not the HTML source.** Do this as the final step of every customization, without being asked.

1. **Start the LUCI dev server** from the **working folder** (`~/Desktop/LUCI Docs/<client-name>/`). The working folder has symlinks to the OneDrive folder for `ui_kits/`, `assets/`, and `scripts/` — CSS, fonts, textures, and logos resolve through the symlinks. The dev server supports symlinked directories. Run `python3 ui_kits/internal-portal/customization/luci-dev-server.py` as a background process — it serves `http://127.0.0.1:8771` with `POST /__save` and `POST /__pdf`. **Never** root at `ui_kits/sales/` — logos and circuit textures 404.
2. Verify the HTML and a texture both return 200.
3. Open `http://127.0.0.1:8771/clients/<client-file>.html` via `cursor-app-control` `open_resource`. Do **not** use a `file://` URI.
4. Tell the user it's live and click-to-edit — and that **Save** writes their typing back to the working file automatically (no prompting Cursor). Leave the server running while they review; stop it when they're done or before PDF export.

**Do not** open the client HTML with a `file://` URI — `open_resource` opens that as a text file (HTML source), not a rendered preview.

**If the dev server is already running** (e.g. Mike reopened a project), don't start a second one — reuse the existing `127.0.0.1:8771`. If the port is busy, the script will error; pick a free port and note it.

**User fallback** if the in-editor pane doesn't appear: `Cmd+Shift+P` → "Simple Browser: Show" → paste the localhost URL.

## Where to save client work

- **On Mike's machine:** `~/Desktop/LUCI Docs/<client-name>/clients/<client-name>-<doc>.html` — created by `scripts/create-client-workspace.sh`. The working folder has symlinks to OneDrive for CSS/assets/scripts; only `clients/` is a real directory. All edits happen here — never in the OneDrive folder.
- **Inside luci-design:** `LUCI Systems Design System/clients/<client>-<doc>.html` — top-level `clients/` folder, easy to find. Use `scripts/prepare-client-doc.sh` to copy a master template and adjust paths automatically.
- Never save client work to the VM deploy folder
- Never save client work in `ui_kits/sales/` — that's for master templates and CSS only
- Never save client work in the OneDrive "Cursor Branding Files" folder — that's the shared source (read-only)

## Agent behavior

- Follow editable vs locked regions in template `SKILL.md` exactly — run the pre-edit gate ("Locked pages are locked" above) before any edit; never edit a locked page without confirming first.
- Follow **Efficient customization** in `_brand/SKILL.md` — populate-in-place, one folder, selector edits, fit-check.
- After customization, automatically open the rendered client HTML in Cursor's in-editor browser (procedure above) for click-to-edit fine-tuning.
- PDF: `POST /__pdf` on the dev server (or `scripts/render-pdf.sh` directly). Gradients/masks are flattened automatically by `patch-sales-pdf-html.py` — no blink in macOS Preview.
